1
00:00:00,980 --> 00:00:07,580
From a long wooden boat on the Rhine, a friend kept noticing small dimples on the water,

2
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:13,040
beside the boat and ahead of it, mostly when the water was smooth.

3
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:16,430
Some lasted a surprisingly long time.

4
00:00:16,980 --> 00:00:20,710
Each dimple sits on top of a whirlpool.

5
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:29,310
Water going round in a circle needs an inward push, and that push is pressure, lower at the centre.

6
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:34,680
So above the spinning water, the surface sags into a small funnel.

7
00:00:35,230 --> 00:00:41,780
So what keeps a small whirlpool like that together, while everything around it keeps changing?

8
00:00:42,330 --> 00:00:49,690
Finding out takes us through Victorian ships, smoke rings and Jupiter, and ends in a lab in Norway,

9
00:00:49,630 --> 00:00:52,910
reading rivers from dimples like these.

10
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:59,670
Physicists size up a flow in nature with a couple of numbers.

11
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:06,760
The first came from Osborne Reynolds, in Manchester, in 1883.

12
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:11,770
He let a thin streak of dye into water flowing through a glass pipe.

13
00:01:11,710 --> 00:01:15,740
Slowly, the streak stayed a clean line.

14
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:20,471
Faster, it suddenly shivered, burst, and filled the pipe.

15
00:01:21,021 --> 00:01:27,741
What decides it is one ratio: how strongly the moving water carries itself along,

16
00:01:27,681 --> 00:01:32,771
compared with how strongly its own friction holds it back.

17
00:01:32,711 --> 00:01:39,261
That's the Reynolds number: speed, times size, divided by viscosity.

18
00:01:39,811 --> 00:01:44,361
In Reynolds' pipe, the switch came at a few thousand.

19
00:01:44,301 --> 00:01:49,721
A river a few metres deep, flowing at a metre or two per second,

20
00:01:49,661 --> 00:01:53,281
has a Reynolds number in the millions.

21
00:01:53,221 --> 00:01:55,491
It's thoroughly turbulent.

22
00:01:56,041 --> 00:01:59,371
The second number came from ships.

23
00:01:59,311 --> 00:02:07,471
In the eighteen-seventies, the engineer William Froude towed model hulls through a long tank at Torquay,

24
00:02:07,411 --> 00:02:11,281
to predict the power a real ship would need.

25
00:02:11,221 --> 00:02:19,061
To make the right waves, a model's speed, divided by the square root of gravity times its length,

26
00:02:19,001 --> 00:02:21,541
has to match the real ship.

27
00:02:22,091 --> 00:02:27,791
That ratio is now called the Froude number, and there's a neat way to see it:

28
00:02:27,731 --> 00:02:33,021
it compares the current's speed with the speed of the surface's own long waves.

29
00:02:33,571 --> 00:02:39,921
At a low Froude number, a long wave can still creep upstream against the current.

30
00:02:39,861 --> 00:02:48,081
Turn the current up past a Froude number of one, and it outruns every such wave: nothing travels upstream.

31
00:02:48,631 --> 00:02:55,491
A river flowing a metre and a half per second over four metres of water, for example,

32
00:02:55,431 --> 00:02:58,771
has a Froude number of about a quarter.

33
00:02:58,711 --> 00:03:04,171
Gravity wins, and the surface stays nearly level, like a lid.

34
00:03:04,721 --> 00:03:08,521
That doesn't make the water beneath it flat.

35
00:03:08,461 --> 00:03:13,211
Water can still rise and sink under a level surface.

36
00:03:13,151 --> 00:03:21,131
But in the ocean and the atmosphere, layering and rotation really do squash vertical motion:

37
00:03:21,071 --> 00:03:28,141
G. I. Taylor found that in a rapidly spinning tank, a whole column of water can move as one.

38
00:03:28,691 --> 00:03:36,931
So physicists often reach for a model: flow that moves only sideways, the same at every depth.

39
00:03:36,871 --> 00:03:40,661
It will turn out to be powerful, and incomplete.

40
00:03:42,611 --> 00:03:51,431
In 1922, the meteorologist Lewis Fry Richardson summed up ordinary turbulence in a rhyme:

41
00:03:51,371 --> 00:03:58,241
big whorls have little whorls that feed on their velocity, and little whorls have lesser whorls,

42
00:03:58,181 --> 00:04:00,781
and so on to viscosity.

43
00:04:01,331 --> 00:04:08,201
Energy tumbles from large eddies to smaller ones, until friction turns it into heat.

44
00:04:08,141 --> 00:04:13,941
Andrei Kolmogorov made that cascade precise in 1941.

45
00:04:14,491 --> 00:04:17,441
Its engine is stretching.

46
00:04:17,381 --> 00:04:22,961
Stretch a spinning tube of fluid, and it gets thinner and spins faster,

47
00:04:22,901 --> 00:04:26,361
like a skater pulling in their arms.

48
00:04:26,301 --> 00:04:31,951
Stretch it to three times its length, and it spins three times as fast.

49
00:04:32,501 --> 00:04:40,951
In the sideways-only model, there's nothing to stretch: a whirl's spin is simply carried along.

50
00:04:40,891 --> 00:04:44,231
And the cascade changes character.

51
00:04:44,171 --> 00:04:52,711
Robert Kraichnan and others showed that energy now climbs to larger scales: small eddies merge into big ones,

52
00:04:52,651 --> 00:04:57,051
while the spin gets drawn out into ever finer filaments.

53
00:04:57,601 --> 00:05:04,991
You can see it in soap films, about the flattest fluid we can make, and on a planetary scale.

54
00:05:04,931 --> 00:05:10,461
Jupiter's weather lives in a thin, fast-spinning, layered shell.

55
00:05:10,401 --> 00:05:17,442
Its Great Red Spot, still wider than the Earth and in the habit of swallowing smaller storms,

56
00:05:17,382 --> 00:05:21,692
has an observing record going back to 1831.

57
00:05:22,242 --> 00:05:30,462
In the ocean, lenses of salty Mediterranean water, called meddies, spin quietly for years.

58
00:05:31,012 --> 00:05:35,922
So in the flat model, whirlpools last and grow.

59
00:05:35,862 --> 00:05:41,432
But what is a dimple, really, and why does it sit on a whirlpool at all?

60
00:05:43,382 --> 00:05:45,312
Back to the dimple.

61
00:05:45,862 --> 00:05:53,252
In a simple model of a whirlpool's core, its depth depends on two things that really vary.

62
00:05:53,192 --> 00:05:56,692
Call the whirlpool's strength gamma.

63
00:05:56,632 --> 00:06:00,942
Double it, and the dip gets four times deeper.

64
00:06:00,882 --> 00:06:08,712
Double the width of its core, as an ageing whirlpool slowly does, and the dip falls to a quarter.

65
00:06:09,262 --> 00:06:16,462
That funnel is also a lens: in shallow water, it casts a dark shadow on the bed,

66
00:06:16,402 --> 00:06:18,312
with a bright edge.

67
00:06:18,862 --> 00:06:21,812
Gamma is the circulation.

68
00:06:21,752 --> 00:06:29,782
Walk a loop in the water, adding up how fast the water flows along your path, step by step.

69
00:06:29,722 --> 00:06:36,572
Well outside the core, double the loop's radius: the water is about half as fast,

70
00:06:36,512 --> 00:06:41,452
but the walk is twice as long, so the total barely changes.

71
00:06:42,002 --> 00:06:46,032
From above, a whirlpool looks like a point.

72
00:06:45,972 --> 00:06:51,542
But what spins is really a line, running down into the river.

73
00:06:51,482 --> 00:06:56,692
A line meets the surface in a single point, and that point is the dimple.

74
00:06:57,242 --> 00:07:06,222
In 1858, Hermann von Helmholtz worked out the rules these vortex lines obey, and one is striking:

75
00:07:06,162 --> 00:07:10,712
a tube of spin can't just end inside the water.

76
00:07:10,652 --> 00:07:13,032
Suppose it did.

77
00:07:12,972 --> 00:07:19,312
Slide a loop past the end without crossing it, and its circulation can't change.

78
00:07:19,252 --> 00:07:23,182
Now shrink the loop to a point: zero.

79
00:07:23,122 --> 00:07:29,292
So the tube must close into a ring, or end on the riverbed or the surface.

80
00:07:29,842 --> 00:07:33,873
Helmholtz's work had an unexpected sequel.

81
00:07:33,813 --> 00:07:39,733
In Edinburgh, Peter Guthrie Tait built a box that puffed out smoke rings,

82
00:07:39,673 --> 00:07:45,013
and showed them to his friend William Thomson, later Lord Kelvin.

83
00:07:44,953 --> 00:07:50,623
The rings bounced off each other, wobbled, and survived their collisions.

84
00:07:51,173 --> 00:07:53,743
Kelvin was captivated.

85
00:07:53,683 --> 00:08:01,363
By Helmholtz's rules, a vortex in a perfect fluid can never be created or destroyed,

86
00:08:01,303 --> 00:08:05,303
so a smoke ring there would last forever.

87
00:08:05,243 --> 00:08:13,873
Perhaps, Kelvin thought, atoms were knotted vortex rings in the ether, each kind of knot a different element.

88
00:08:14,423 --> 00:08:16,873
The theory was wrong.

89
00:08:16,813 --> 00:08:23,323
But Tait spent years drawing up tables of knots, hoping to match them to the elements,

90
00:08:23,263 --> 00:08:27,943
and those tables helped start the mathematics of knot theory.

91
00:08:28,493 --> 00:08:33,713
Kelvin also gave the rule its cleanest form: in a perfect fluid,

92
00:08:33,653 --> 00:08:39,103
circulation around a loop moving with the water never changes.

93
00:08:39,043 --> 00:08:42,973
A big loop far from a paddle stays at zero,

94
00:08:42,913 --> 00:08:48,223
so the paddle's spin comes in equal amounts both ways.

95
00:08:48,163 --> 00:08:55,463
Bend a tube into an arch with both feet on the surface, and you see it: two dimples,

96
00:08:55,403 --> 00:08:57,963
spinning opposite ways.

97
00:08:58,513 --> 00:09:03,983
Each rides the other's swirl, so the pair sails off together.

98
00:09:03,923 --> 00:09:07,493
Bring them closer, and they go faster.

99
00:09:09,443 --> 00:09:13,423
So where do the dimples on the Rhine come from?

100
00:09:13,363 --> 00:09:18,203
Probably not paddles: there were hardly any boats.

101
00:09:18,143 --> 00:09:21,793
The likeliest source is the riverbed.

102
00:09:21,733 --> 00:09:28,883
Water touching it is held still, while a little way above, the current races past.

103
00:09:28,823 --> 00:09:35,093
That shear is spin: a paddle wheel lying on the bed would turn.

104
00:09:35,643 --> 00:09:39,983
River engineers have long described the results.

105
00:09:39,923 --> 00:09:49,033
In 1947, Gerard Matthes wrote about kolks, violent upward surges from the beds of big rivers,

106
00:09:48,973 --> 00:09:55,933
and the boils they raise at the surface: smooth, bulging patches of upwelling water.

107
00:09:56,483 --> 00:10:04,333
Near a wall, researchers came to picture the spin rolling up into arches, called hairpin vortices,

108
00:10:04,273 --> 00:10:07,663
lifting away from the bed in bursts.

109
00:10:08,213 --> 00:10:14,853
When a rising arch reaches the surface, its head doesn't simply get sliced off.

110
00:10:14,793 --> 00:10:22,083
Friction lets the vortex reconnect to the surface, and its two legs become a pair of dimples.

111
00:10:22,633 --> 00:10:27,473
Simulations since the nineteen-nineties show what those ends do.

112
00:10:27,413 --> 00:10:30,883
Right at the surface, they stand upright,

113
00:10:30,823 --> 00:10:36,413
and they can wander across it while the tube below is twisted by the turbulence.

114
00:10:36,963 --> 00:10:41,403
So a surface whirlpool is caught between two effects.

115
00:10:41,343 --> 00:10:45,323
Friction spreads its core and weakens it.

116
00:10:45,263 --> 00:10:51,523
Water converging and sinking beneath it stretches it and spins it back up.

117
00:10:51,463 --> 00:11:01,863
Laboratory experiments on a nearly flat surface have watched both: vortices strengthening during downwellings, then spreading out after them.

118
00:11:02,413 --> 00:11:05,663
How fast does friction work?

119
00:11:05,603 --> 00:11:13,743
The spreading takes a time proportional to the core's width squared, divided by the viscosity: double the width,

120
00:11:13,683 --> 00:11:20,593
and spreading takes four times as long, though a wider core also starts out shallower.

121
00:11:20,533 --> 00:11:27,383
Water at five degrees is about one and a half times as viscous as water at twenty,

122
00:11:27,323 --> 00:11:30,893
so friction works that much faster in winter.

123
00:11:31,443 --> 00:11:34,783
Seeing one is a separate question.

124
00:11:34,723 --> 00:11:39,553
A dimple is shallow, not much steeper than a small ripple,

125
00:11:39,493 --> 00:11:44,273
so wind ripples can hide a whirlpool that is still there.

126
00:11:44,213 --> 00:11:48,623
Behind a motorboat, its own wake roughens the water.

127
00:11:48,563 --> 00:11:54,043
Ahead and beside it, dimples show up best on smooth water.

128
00:11:54,593 --> 00:11:57,923
So what keeps a small whirlpool together?

129
00:11:57,863 --> 00:12:04,783
It is the end of a vortex line that can't just stop, standing upright at the surface,

130
00:12:04,723 --> 00:12:11,223
spun up now and then by sinking water, and fading only slowly by friction.

131
00:12:13,173 --> 00:12:17,453
There's one more surprise, and it lives in the flat model.

132
00:12:18,003 --> 00:12:20,293
Think of a pendulum.

133
00:12:20,233 --> 00:12:27,743
Plot its angle across and its momentum up, and its whole state becomes a single moving point.

134
00:12:27,683 --> 00:12:35,283
Draw the contours of its energy, and the point follows a contour, rather than rolling downhill.

135
00:12:35,223 --> 00:12:42,273
That's Hamilton's rule, the backbone of mechanics, and it has a famous consequence:

136
00:12:42,213 --> 00:12:47,423
a patch of starting states can stretch, but never changes its area.

137
00:12:47,973 --> 00:12:56,004
In the sideways-only model, water can't be squashed, so patches of it keep their area too.

138
00:12:55,944 --> 00:12:58,574
That's no coincidence.

139
00:12:58,514 --> 00:13:05,664
A speck of water follows exactly Hamilton's rule, on a landscape called the stream function.

140
00:13:06,214 --> 00:13:11,384
With one steady whirlpool, the speck follows a contour.

141
00:13:11,324 --> 00:13:19,614
When the whirlpools move, the landscape changes beneath it, but the same rule still sets its motion.

142
00:13:19,554 --> 00:13:23,954
In this model, the surface is the speck's phase space.

143
00:13:24,504 --> 00:13:34,164
In 1876, Gustav Kirchhoff showed that the whirlpools themselves obey it: each is carried by the others' swirl,

144
00:13:34,104 --> 00:13:41,144
and together they follow the slopes of one landscape, with each whirlpool's strength as a weight.

145
00:13:41,694 --> 00:13:45,044
Three whirlpools dance regularly.

146
00:13:44,984 --> 00:13:53,994
Four can turn chaotic: in these particular runs, started a micron apart, they part ways within seconds.

147
00:13:55,944 --> 00:14:02,444
Now put back what the flat model left out: water rising and sinking underneath.

148
00:14:02,994 --> 00:14:08,154
A real patch of surface water doesn't have to keep its area.

149
00:14:08,094 --> 00:14:14,044
It spreads where water wells up from below, and shrinks where water sinks away.

150
00:14:13,984 --> 00:14:20,444
Incompressibility keeps volume; only in the flat model does it keep area.

151
00:14:20,994 --> 00:14:30,094
And that spreading and shrinking is what a group in Trondheim, Norway, around Omer Babiker and Simen Ellingsen,

152
00:14:30,034 --> 00:14:32,944
has been reading from dimples.

153
00:14:32,884 --> 00:14:41,044
In their simulations, the number of dimples in a region tracked how strongly its surface was spreading and converging,

154
00:14:40,984 --> 00:14:44,324
closely, with a short delay.

155
00:14:44,264 --> 00:14:52,074
This year, a laboratory tank experiment, at much higher Reynolds numbers, found the same kind of link.

156
00:14:52,624 --> 00:14:57,934
That matters, because the same stirring renews the skin of the water,

157
00:14:57,874 --> 00:15:05,374
and that helps set how fast gases like oxygen and carbon dioxide cross between a river and the air.

158
00:15:05,924 --> 00:15:11,574
So those dimples beside the boat weren't only whirlpools that happened to last.

159
00:15:11,514 --> 00:15:19,014
Each was a small mark on the surface, connected to a hidden, three-dimensional flow underneath.

160
00:15:18,954 --> 00:15:26,034
And a friend who noticed how long some of them lasted was already, in a way, reading it.
